    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
      <p>This publication discusses two sets of rules: that may limit the losses you can deduct on your tax return from any trade, business, rental, or other income-producing activity. The first part of the publication contains the passive activity rules. The second part discusses the at-risk rules. However, when you figure out your allowable losses from any activity, you must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Passive Activity Activities</h2>
    <p>Passive activity credits. You can subtract passive activity credits only from the tax year's passive activity income. You can also include the general business credit and other credits in your passive activity income.</p>
    <p>Real estate activities. Certain real estate activities are considered passive activities. These include rental activities, and certain activities that are not considered passive activities.</p>
    <p>Passive activity losses. You can deduct passive activity losses only from passive activity income. You may deduct passive activity losses from nonpassive activity income only to the extent that you have passive activity income.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <div class="section-title">Get forms and other information faster and easier by:</div>
      <div style="margin-top: 10px;">
        <p>Computer • <span data-field="website_url">{{ website_url }}</span> or FTP • <span data-field="ftp_url">{{ ftp_url }}</span></p>
        <p>FAX • <span data-field="fax_number">{{ fax_number }}</span> (from your fax machine)</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Passive Activities</div>
      <p>There are two kinds of passive activities: trade or business activities and rental activities. Trade or business activities are activities in which you materially participate. Rental activities are activities that are not considered passive activities.</p>
      <p>Treatment of former passive activities. A former passive activity is an activity that was passive in a prior year, but is not passive in the current year. You can deduct a prior year unallocated passive activity loss from the current year's passive activity income.</p>
      <p>Significant Participation Passive Activities. A significant participation passive activity is an activity in which you materially participate for more than 100 hours during the tax year, and you have an interest in the activity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Activities That Are Not Passive</h2>
    <p>The following are not passive activities:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Trade or business activities in which you materially participate.</li>
      <li>Rental activities in which you materially participate.</li>
      <li>Activities in which you materially participate.</li>
      <li>Activities in which you materially participate.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>See Section 1.469-1T(e)(6).</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <div class="section-title">Material Participation</div>
      <p>A trade or business activity is not a passive activity if you materially participate in the activity. Material participation means that you participate in the activity for more than 500 hours during the tax year.</p>
      <p>You must determine whether you materially participate in the activity. You can use any of the following tests:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>You participate in the activity for more than 500 hours during the tax year.</li>
        <li>You substantially all of the participation in the activity during the tax year.</li>
        <li>Your participation in the activity is greater than the participation of any other individual in the activity.</li>
        <li>You qualify as a full-time employee of the business.</li>
        <li>You substantially all of the participation in the activity during the tax year.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you meet any of the exceptions to the material participation rules, you may be able to treat the activity as nonpassive.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Passive Activity Deductions</div>
      <p>Passive activity deductions include all deductions from activities that are passive activities. You can deduct passive activity losses only from passive activity income. You may deduct passive activity losses from nonpassive activity income only to the extent that you have passive activity income.</p>
      <p>Deductions for expenses other than those allowed for tax purposes. You cannot deduct expenses that are not allowed for tax purposes. You cannot deduct expenses that are not allowed for tax purposes.</p>
      <p>Capital loss carryovers. You cannot carry over capital losses from passive activities.</p>
      <p>Net operating loss deductions. You cannot deduct net operating losses from passive activities.</p>
      <p>Other limitations. You cannot deduct other losses from passive activities.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Real Estate Rental Activities</h2>
    <p>A real estate rental activity is a passive activity if you do not materially participate in the activity. You may be able to treat the activity as nonpassive if you qualify as a real estate professional.</p>
    <p>Real estate professionals. You may qualify as a real estate professional if you meet certain tests. These tests require you to meet certain tests.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <div class="section-title">Trade or Business Activities</div>
      <p>A trade or business activity is an activity that:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Involves the conduct of a trade or business.</li>
        <li>Is not a passive activity.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>You must determine whether the activity is a trade or business activity. You can use the following tests:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>You materially participate in the activity.</li>
        <li>You are engaged in the activity as a principal trade or business.</li>
        <li>You are engaged in the activity as a principal trade or business.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you are engaged in the activity as a principal trade or business, you may be able to treat the activity as nonpassive.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Passive Activity Income</div>
      <p>Passive activity income. Passive activity income is income from a passive activity. You can deduct passive activity losses only from passive activity income.</p>
      <p>Passive activity income includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rental income.</li>
        <li>Income from a trade or business activity that is passive.</li>
        <li>Income from a partnership interest in a passive activity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Passive activity income does not include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Income from a trade or business activity that is not passive.</li>
        <li>Income from a partnership interest in a nonpassive activity.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Grouping Your Activities</h2>
    <p>You can group certain activities together to determine whether they are passive or nonpassive. This is called grouping.</p>
    <p>Grouping rules. You can group certain activities together if they meet certain requirements. These requirements include:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The activities are related.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted by the same taxpayer.</li>
      <li>The activities are conducted in the same location.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you group activities, you must treat them as a single activity for passive activity purposes.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <div class="section-title">At-Risk Limits</div>
      <p>The at-risk rules determine whether you have sufficient at-risk amount to cover losses from an activity. If you do not have sufficient at-risk amount, you cannot deduct the loss.</p>
      <p>Activities covered by the at-risk rules. The at-risk rules apply to certain activities, including:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rental activities.</li>
        <li>Activities in which you materially participate.</li>
        <li>Activities in which you do not materially participate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>At-risk amounts. The at-risk amount is the amount of money you have personally invested in the activity. This includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cash contributions.</li>
        <li>Property contributions.</li>
        <li>Loans you have made to the activity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Amounts not at risk. Amounts that are not at risk include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Loans made to the activity by others.</li>
        <li>Guarantees you have made for the activity.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Recharacterization of Passive Income</div>
      <p>Recharacterization allows you to change the character of income or loss. This is usually done when a taxpayer's circumstances change.</p>
      <p>Recharacterization of passive income. If you have passive activity income, but your circumstances change, you may be able to recharacterize some of that income to nonpassive income.</p>
      <p>Recharacterization of passive losses. If you have passive activity losses, but your circumstances change, you may be able to recharacterize some of those losses to nonpassive losses.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 24px;">Comprehensive Example</h2>
    <p>This example illustrates how the passive activity rules and the at-risk rules work together. It shows how to determine passive activity income and losses, and how to apply the at-risk limitations.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <div class="section-title">Dispositions</div>
      <p>A disposition is the sale or exchange of an interest in an activity. When you dispose of an interest, you must determine how the income or loss from that interest is treated.</p>
      <p>Dispositions of property. When you sell property, you must determine how the gain or loss is treated. This depends on whether the property was held in a passive activity.</p>
      <p>Dispositions of partnership interests. When you sell your interest in a partnership, you must determine how the gain or loss is treated. This depends on whether the partnership was a passive activity.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">How To Report Your Passive Activity Loss</div>
      <p>You must report passive activity losses on Form <span data-field="form_number">{{ form_number }}</span>. This form is used to report losses from passive activities.</p>
      <p>You must also report passive activity income and losses on your tax return. You must report these items on your tax return.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

