<div class="page">
  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">At-Risk Limits</h3>
  <p>The at-risk rules limit your losses from most activities to your amount at risk in the activity. You treat any loss that is disallowed by the at-risk rules as a suspended loss and you can deduct it in a future year if your amount at risk is reduced below your loss.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 20px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <p>You must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules, as discussed in the first part of this publication.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <p>1 Stock owned directly or indirectly by or for a corporation, partnership, estate, or trust, or by its shareholders, partners, or beneficiaries.</p>
      <p>2 An individual is considered to own the stock owned directly or indirectly by or for a corporation, partnership, estate, or trust, or by its shareholders, partners, or beneficiaries.</p>
      <p>3 If a person holds an option to buy stock, the option is considered to be owned by the person.</p>
      <p>4 When applying rule (1), or (2), or (3) as treated above, the amount at risk is the amount of money the person has invested or is personally liable for the debt of the corporation, partnership, estate, or trust.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Loss defined. A loss is the excess of allowable deductions from the activity over the gross income from the activity. If the loss is disallowed by the at-risk rules, it is suspended and carried forward to the next tax year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 20px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <p>5 Stock that may be considered owned by an individual under rule (2) or (3) is considered to be owned by the individual under rule (2) or (3) as treated above.</p>
      <p>6 A corporation and a partnership are considered to be owned by the individual if the individual owns more than 50% of the stock or is otherwise considered to be owned by the individual.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <p>Exception for holding real property placed in service before 1987. The at-risk rules do not apply to losses from real property placed in service before 1987. This exception does not apply to the holding of an interest in a partnership or S corporation that is engaged in holding real property placed in service before 1987.</p>
      <p>Personal property and services that are incidental to the operation of a business are not considered to be at risk in the activity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>7 Stock that may be considered owned by an individual under rule (2) or (3) is considered to be owned by the individual under rule (2) or (3) as treated above.</p>
    <p>8 A corporation and a partnership are considered to be owned by the individual if the individual owns more than 50% of the stock or is otherwise considered to be owned by the individual.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Activities Covered by the At-Risk Rules</p>
    <p>If you are involved in one of the following activities, the at-risk rules apply to your losses from that activity:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Farming.</li>
      <li>Exploring for, or exploiting, oil and gas.</li>
      <li>Mining, quarrying, or drilling for minerals or other natural resources.</li>
      <li>Leasing section 1245 property, including personal property and certain other tangible property, that is depreciable or amortizable.</li>
      <li>Exploring for, or exploiting, geothermal energy.</li>
      <li>Any other activity not included in (1) through (5) that is carried on as a trade or business for the production of income.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Section 1245 property. Section 1245 property includes tangible property that is held for the production of income. The at-risk rules apply to losses from activities involving section 1245 property.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>At-Risk Amounts</strong></p>
    <p>You are at risk in any activity if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The money you have invested in the activity.</li>
      <li>Any amounts borrowed to you for the use in the activity.</li>
      <li>Any amounts you are personally liable for repaying.</li>
      <li>Any amounts you have contributed to the activity.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p><strong>Aggregations of Activities</strong></p>
    <p>Activities described in (6) under Activities Covered by the At-Risk Rules, are aggregated for at-risk purposes.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>You actively participate in the management of the trade or business, or</li>
      <li>The trade or business is carried on by a partnership or S corporation and you are a partner or shareholder, or</li>
      <li>The trade or business is carried on by a corporation and you are an officer or director.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">What is Affected?</h3>
  <p>The at-risk limits apply to individuals (includ-ing partners and S corporation shareholders) and to certain closely held corporations (other than S corporations).</p>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Closely held corporation. For the at-risk rules, a corporation is a closely held corporation if 80% or more of the stock is owned directly or indirectly by five or fewer individuals, or if the stock is owned by five or fewer individuals, and the stock is not owned by a publicly traded corporation.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Effect of government price support programs. A government price support program is a program that provides a price floor or other support to a producer. Losses from activities affected by such programs are not limited by the at-risk rules.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Effect of increasing amounts at risk in an activity. If your amount at risk in an activity increases in a later year, you may deduct losses from that activity in the later year, even if you did not have enough at-risk amount in the earlier year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Amounts Not At Risk</p>
    <p>You are not considered at risk for amounts borrowed to you for the use in an activity if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The amount is borrowed by you in connection with the activity of holding real property.</li>
      <li>The amount is borrowed by you in connection with the activity of holding personal property.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Nonrecourse financing. Nonrecourse financing is financing for which you are not personally liable. If you borrow money to finance an activity, you are at risk for the amount of money you have invested in the activity unless the loan is secured by real property.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Related persons. Related persons include:</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Members of a family, but only brothers and sisters, spouses, ancestors, descendants, and in-laws.</li>
    <li>Two corporations that are members of the same group of corporations controlled by a single person.</li>
    <li>The fiduciary or the fiduciary of a trust.</li>
    <li>A corporation and an individual who own more than 50% of the stock of the corporation.</li>
    <li>A corporation and an individual who own more than 10% of the stock of the corporation.</li>
    <li>A partnership and a person who owns more than 50% of the partnership interest.</li>
    <li>A partnership and a person who owns more than 10% of the partnership interest.</li>
  </ol>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Reductions of Amounts At Risk</p>
    <p>You may reduce your amount at risk in any activity if:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The amount is borrowed by you in connection with the activity of holding real property.</li>
      <li>The amount is borrowed by you in connection with the activity of holding personal property.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Receipt Rule</p>
    <p>You must recapture at least part of your prior year's loss from the activity when you receive income from the activity in the current year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <p>Pre-1979 activity. If the amount you had at risk in an activity at the end of the tax year prior to 1979 is less than the amount of the loss from the activity, you may deduct the loss in the current year, even if you did not have enough at-risk amount in the prior year.</p>
  </div>
